<?nvs
// Introspection, as a program: a class described by itself, a member the
// description will not open, the one question only a `mixed` can be asked,
// and source read as data.
//
// [ADR 0019](/docs/decisions/0019.md)
// makes both of these built in rather than extension-provided, and gives
// them the two rules the third and fourth lines below exist for.
//
// **Reflection enforces the same visibility and hook checks ordinary code
// does.** A `private` member is not reachable through `Core\Reflect` from
// outside its class, because a reflection API that quietly lifts the rules
// is a way of writing code that the type checker cannot see — which is the
// half PHP's `setAccessible` gave away. Naming a member is the program's
// shape and always answerable, so `properties` lists all three; reading
// one is an act, checked where the call is written, so
// `readableProperties` answers two from out here and the read of the
// third is refused.
//
// **A parsed AST is inert typed data**, with no path back into execution.
// `Core\Ast::parse` is a reader, not an `eval` behind a longer name; ADR
// 0052 keeps that door shut and this is the member that would otherwise
// have propped it open.
//
// The five lines this prints are frozen by an exact check in a goal record
// under `data/goals/`; the source is not.

class Point
{
    public int $x = 3;
    public int $y = 4;
    private int $scratch = 0;
}

var $point = new Point();
var $described = Core\Reflect::forObject($point);
echo 'class=', $described->name(), "\n";

// The visibility-respecting walk: two of the three, seen from out here.
// `properties()` beside it is the complete roster, and the difference
// between the two answers is exactly what `get` refuses below.
echo 'members=', Core\Arr::count($described->readableProperties()), "\n";

// And the third, asked for by name. The clause is `RuntimeError` rather
// than `Throwable`, so a misspelled member would not have been counted as
// a visibility refusal.
try {
    var $hidden = $described->get($point, 'scratch');
    echo 'private read', "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo 'private refused', "\n";
}

// `typeOf` is the single replacement for PHP's fourteen `is_*` predicates
// plus `gettype`, and it is only meaningful here: on anything but a
// `mixed`, the checker already knows the answer and this call would be a
// constant.
mixed $value = 7;
string $kind = match (Core\Reflect::typeOf($value)) {
    Core\Reflect\TypeKind::Int => 'int',
    Core\Reflect\TypeKind::Float => 'float',
    Core\Reflect\TypeKind::Text => 'string',
    default => 'other',
};
echo 'kind=', $kind, "\n";

// Source as data. Nothing below runs the program it just read.
var $tree = Core\Ast::parse('<?nvs echo 1 + 2;');
echo 'parsed ', Core\Arr::count($tree->nodes()), " nodes\n";
